1958 0 obj
<<
  /Type /StructElem
  /S /Figure
  /P 1957 0 R
  /Alt (This diagram illustrates the geometric principles of Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) imaging and how mountainous terrain influences radar signal acquisition and introduces specific distortions. The diagram features two main components: 1. A greyscale SAR amplitude image, identified as a Sentinel-1 image, showing a mountainous region. The image axes are labelled `azimuth` (horizontal) and `range` (vertical, decreasing downwards). A white line segment labelled A-A' is superimposed on this image, indicating a cross-section. 2. A cross-sectional diagram beneath the image, depicting a terrain profile between points A and A' on the `Ground Range` axis. This profile represents the land features corresponding to the A-A' line in the SAR image. A diagonal line, labelled `Slant Range`, represents the path of the radar signal from the SAR sensor. Parallel lines extend from the terrain profile to the `Slant Range` line, illustrating how different parts of the ground are mapped. The `Slant Range` line is segmented into blocks, each representing a distinct radar phenomenon resulting from the terrain's interaction with the radar signal: * A light grey block represents `FORESHORTENING`, which occurs when the radar beam illuminates the near side of a slope, causing objects to appear compressed in the range direction. * White blocks represent `LAY-OVER`, occurring when the top of a feature is closer to the sensor than its base, leading to the feature appearing 'laid over' its base in the radar image. Two `LAY-OVER` sections are depicted. * A black block represents `SHADOW`, which occurs when parts of the terrain are completely obscured from the radar beam, resulting in no signal return. This diagram visually connects the physical terrain profile on the `Ground Range` to the resulting radar signal acquisition along the `Slant Range`, highlighting these inherent geometric distortions crucial for interpreting SAR data from systems like Sentinel-1, which are used in services such as the European Ground Motion Service (EGMS).)
  /A [<<
    /O /Layout
    /Placement /Block
  >>]
  /K [3]
  /Pg 2937 0 R
>>
endobj

